artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider conformal field theories (CFTs) in d-dimensional Euclidean space that are enriched by the presence of a p-dimensional conformal defect (with p < d), including the special case p = d-1 of a boundary (BCFT). Such a defect preserves a subgroup SO(p+1,1) × SO(d-p) of the ambient conformal group SO(d+1,1) and gives rise to two structures: (i) a "bulk" channel in which local operators in the ambient CFT have an operator-product expansion onto operators living on the defect, and (ii) a "defect" channel in which operators on the defect carry their own OPE. Standard open questions in this setting include classifying the conformal defects of a given bulk CFT, computing defect operator spectra and defect-bulk OPE coefficients, understanding boundary/defect entropies and their monotonicity under defect renormalization-group flows, and characterizing the constraints imposed by crossing symmetry on bulk one-point functions and bulk-to-defect two-point functions.

Determine to what extent each of the following techniques can be developed into an effective, systematic tool for answering such structural questions about boundary and defect CFTs:

(a) supersymmetry (e.g., superconformal Ward identities, localization on manifolds with boundary or defects, defect indices);
(b) the numerical and analytic conformal bootstrap, applied to the mixed system of bulk and defect crossing equations;
(c) integrability, in particular for defects in planar N = 4 super Yang–Mills and related integrable models;
(d) topological field theory, including the use of fully extended TQFTs and topological defects to organize symmetry categories and defect data.

Specifically, formulate criteria of "effectiveness" (e.g., yielding rigorous bounds on defect CFT data, exact computations of defect observables, or classification results for conformal defects) and decide for which classes of boundary/defect CFTs each of (a)–(d) meets these criteria.

